From aeb06fd9dc476772263c12f74b2b1c561bf0abf9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(effects): transport native resources and sound commands

Use the preceding sound wire codecs as the protocol prerequisite. Own the versioned rendering capability with accepted native resources, including proxy sessions. The format and Bedrock protocol of the accepted-source section in pack.mcmeta mark that capability, so the pack needs no separate marker file.

Native 1.26.51.1 captures on protocol 2193 establish that PlaySound uses
signed eighth-block positions and signed loop counts. UpdateSoundData
carries a 64-bit handle followed by seven tagged variants. Consume all
seven fields and use the last variant, matching the shared native member.
Fade serializes duration before target volume.

Route ordered sound commands to clients advertising the native playback
channel. Include mapped Java fallbacks so unavailable licensed assets do
not prevent playback or joining. Ordinary Java clients retain positioned
sound translation; their protocol cannot express per-instance controls.

Carry native sound definitions and samples in the converted resource
pack, preserving pack order through ViaProxy. Bound archive entries,
paths, and decompressed resources, and keep generated ZIP bytes stable.

Test coordinate precision, signed loops, optional fields, all seven
update variants, wire alignment, archive overrides, and invalid paths.

Include server WAV samples in the native resource archive and decode them in
core so direct and ViaProxy clients share one bounded asset processor. The
1.26.51.1 extension initializer at 0x140275490 lists FSB, OGG, then WAV.
Microsoft documents all three Bedrock formats. Parse RIFF chunks and padding
before allocating PCM; support integer and floating-point PCM, extensible
precision, and mono/stereo IMA ADPCM with final-block sample counts. Bound
encoded and decoded sizes and reject truncated or ambiguous sample layouts.
Decoder tests establish numeric and validation behavior. Native mixing, WAV
loop metadata, and multichannel playback remain separate parity work.

IMA truncates each step contribution before addition. Fourteen private
FFmpeg comparisons cover 252,000 scalar samples: integer PCM and IMA are
exact, and floating-point PCM differs by at most one quantization step. Trim
IMA padding to the RIFF fact frame count rather than exposing encoder padding.
These comparisons verify the decoder, not the native client's audible mix.

Expose the native server request listener-range gate in the core PlaySound
model. The 1.26.51.1 client (executable SHA-256
537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7)
checks this gate in function 144719f20 before sound dispatch or captions.
Its float radius is 16 times the supplied volume above one, otherwise 16.
It rejects the radius boundary and unordered distances unless bypass is
set. Preserve coordinate conversion and summation order at float precision
instead of measuring with Java doubles or catalog attenuation settings.

Carry caption translations in the shared bounded, pack-ordered native
resource archive. Keep caption admission, native float direction calculations, localized duplicate
refresh, elapsed-time state, and the one-second quartic fade in core.
The client integration supplies player and listener pose and draws the HUD.

Pinned 1.26.51.1 executable dispatch/storage probes establish filtering and
refresh behavior. Its caption-model update uses wall-clock nanoseconds and
float subtraction. Native HUD resources define a one-second in-quart fade
inside the selected total lifetime. Local actor sounds and complete visible
caption parity still require separate verification.

Share the native float listener gate with local emitters without quantizing
particle positions into PlaySound's fixed-point packet domain. Native
1.26.51.1 local-emitter execution verifies 444 admission cases; the existing
server request gate retains its bypass behavior and coordinate conversion.

Share one bounded, deterministic archive across actors, sounds, captions,
and particles. Preserve every source pack index and texture-only overlay,
including overrides for licensed built-in effects unavailable to the converter.

Particle format evidence comes from pinned 1.26.51.1 resources and the native
graph loader. Reject traversal, wrong protocols, unsupported paths, duplicate
entries and decompressed limits. Keep packet dispatch and final visible
parity separate from archive transport verification.

Transport primary Bedrock block IDs and names to native effect clients so
expiration predicates also work through ViaProxy. Publish compact section
snapshots, ordered updates, unloads and dimension resets, with dictionary
fragments before their referencing data. Late channel registration replays
the current loaded state. Preserve unknown IDs and unloaded native air;
do not infer identifiers from the many-to-one Java block mapping.

The shared client mirror validates protocol, bounds and packed indexes.
Uniform air sections carry no cell array. Targeted tests retain every
coordinate through all bit widths, full palettes, updates, negative
coordinates, empty sections and resets. Live route and particle packet
comparisons remain required.

Embed complete accepted Bedrock ZIPs as .mcpack assets inside the ordinary
Java RP. Keep ordered digests, provenance, selected subpack names, protocol
and base-game version in a pack.mcmeta section that vanilla ignores. Emit
no custom native-library envelope. Reuse unchanged received ZIPs after
checking their expanded contents against a SHA-256 snapshot. Decrypted or
edited sources become complete accepted ZIPs with every source file.

Bound ZIP directories before allocating their entry indexes. Snapshot
ZIP64, prepended and directory-heavy inputs into ordinary bounded ZIPs.
Check each same-pack payload's length and SHA-256 before decoded-cache
reuse, then validate expanded paths, counts, sizes and entry checksums.
Fingerprint complete source context and selection for conversion caches.

Expose complete sources on archive layers for future client parsers. Keep
current actor reconstruction on its established subset so unrelated block
and UI files are not reparsed by every definition constructor. Preserve
Java assets, runtime mappings and resource ownership. Remove the old
actor_library.bin codec and fallback; new packs carry only nested sources.
Tests cover unchanged ZIPs, in-place and selected-view edits, complete
unknown and unselected context, role order, digest failures and malformed
metadata/directories. Native pixel parity remains separate verification.

Share immutable sound-caption translations across native actor libraries.
Overlay only higher-pack language files and search all layers for the
selected locale before English fallback, preserving pack order and empty
translations. An overlay without language files reuses the original table.
This avoids duplicating every bundled locale for each world actor type.
Tests cover structural reuse, overlay priority and locale fallback.

Carry both native block layers, exact runtime state IDs and immutable state properties through the negotiated block_identifiers_v3 channel. Aim-assist discovery checks the extra layer before the primary layer when liquid targeting is enabled; primary-only names cannot reproduce this. Snapshots include native air and both palettes, and incremental updates retain the layer. Keep particle expiration on the primary layer.

Bound dictionary messages by encoded byte size as well as entry count, and bound the retained definition union. Tests cover independent layer replacement and updates, missing all-air palettes, negative and hashed IDs, dimension/forget lifecycles, state properties, and packed/wire boundaries. This supplies authoritative inputs for aim execution; selection geometry, block tags and actual aim targeting remain separate work.

Carry the forward Java liquid state in each native definition. The target
1.26.51.1 selector at 142d80600 chooses any non-air secondary block before
falling back to the primary layer, even when bedrock cannot be waterlogged
in Java. A pinned-binary probe executes the selector and both real layer
getters for 180 primary/secondary, coordinate and chunk-availability cases.
Only chunk-provider and leaf palette-cell lookup are supplied boundaries;
material classification, meshing and movement are outside that probe.

Version the negotiated identity channel and codec together. Keep unknown
native definitions distinct from known nonfluid states. Publish replacement
section arrays through a concurrent world snapshot so client render workers
can query the same authoritative layers without reading mutable arrays or
locking every fluid lookup. Resets replace the complete world atomically.

Translate liquid over native air into its forward Java fluid block in both
full chunks and individual block lookups. There is no primary geometry to
preserve in this case. Leaving it as Java air causes render-section empty
checks to discard the section before client mesh integration can run.
Keep exact native layer identities in the mirror for effects and queries.
Share liquid classification between this translation and its dictionary.

The regression test exercises all four liquid identifiers across 16
mapped depths, full secondary-only sections, dense fluid/non-air counts
and individual removals. Introduce the existing Mockito test dependency
at this first use; later feature patches reuse it.

Index caption locale files without parsing them during resource preparation.
Resolve each requested locale once with thread-safe publication, preserve
pack overrides and inherited-locale-before-English fallback, and snapshot
input bytes so deferred reads retain the eager parser ownership contract.
Use a literal newline splitter to avoid regular-expression parsing work.

Keep actor reconstruction on its established resource subset. Complete
block, UI and future-format files remain in the shared archive source maps.
Feeding those files to every existing definition constructor reparses data
unrelated to actor loading and changes the old dependency boundary. Future
client parsers can read complete sources without changing backend export.

Expose the source index identity and selected-subpack file view for frontend consumers. Keep the actor-only resource view narrow; UI consumers select their files from the complete accepted source.
